<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-03">
  <title>Week 5 and Week 6</title>

    <introduction>
    <p>
      Week 5 started by looking at how functions are constructed, using arithmetic and composition.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>F3 Function Deconstruction</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-F3">
      <title>F3</title>
      <p>
        I can deconstruct a function by recognizing the last operation 
        as a sum, difference, product, quotient, or composition of other expressions.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Deconstruct the function <m>f(x)=\displaystyle \frac{x^2-3x+5}{(2x+1)^4}</m>.
          That is, express <m>f(x)</m> as a sum, product, quotient, or composition of two functions, <m>f_1(x)</m> and <m>f_2(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Repeat the process for each of <m>f_1(x)</m> and <m>f_2(x)</m> as constructed from functions <m>f_3, f_4, f_5, f_6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The last operation in the formula for the function <m>f(x)</m> is <em>division</em>, <m>f(x)</m> is a <term>quotient</term>. The operands are the functions involved in division: <m>f_1(x)=x^2-3x+5</m> and <m>f_2(x)=(2x+1)^4</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We repeat the process for <m>f_1(x)=x^2-3x+5</m>. Because of the minus sign, it looks like subtraction. We could do that but subtraction can be more complicated than thinking of it as addition. For example, we might say <m>f_1(x)</m> is a <term>sum</term> of operands <m>f_3(x)=x^2</m> and <m>f_4(x)=-3x+5</m>. If we did use subtraction, the negative sign would have to distribute, so we would have to say <m>f_1(x)</m> is a <term>difference</term> of <m>f_3(x)=x^2</m> and <m>f_4(x)=3x-5</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also repeat the process for <m>f_2(x)=(2x+1)^4</m>. The last operation is the power acting on a formula. We say <m>f_2(x)</m> is a <term>composition</term> of the functions <m>f_5(x)=x^4</m> (the outer function is the power) and <m>f_6(x)=2x+1</m> (the inner function).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>F4 Function Inverses</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-F4">
      <title>F4</title>
      <p>
        I can use inverse functions to solve equations involving an abstract function.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the fact that unspecified functions <m>A(x)</m> and <m>B(x)</m> are 
          inverses and the equation
          <md>
            \frac{2}{3A(x+4)}-4 = y
          </md>
          to solve for <m>x</m> as a function of <m>y</m> involving the functions <m>A</m> and/or <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We use the method of isolation earlier. When we reach a stage where the last operation on the side of the equation with <m>x</m> is the function operation,
          we apply the inverse function on both sides and the inverse function.
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{2}{3A(x+4)}-4 \amp = y </mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{2}{3A(x+4)} \amp = y+4 </mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{3A(x+4)}{2} \amp = \frac{1}{y+4} </mrow>
            <mrow>A(x+4) \amp = \frac{2}{3(y+4)} </mrow>
            <mrow>B\big(A(x+4)\big) \amp = B\left(\frac{2}{3(y+4)}\right) </mrow>
            <mrow>x+4 \amp = B\left(\frac{2}{3(y+4)}\right) </mrow>
            <mrow>x \amp = B\left(\frac{2}{3(y+4)}\right) - 4 </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection>
    <title>F5 Describing Functions</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-F5">
      <title>F5</title>
      <p>
        I can use the vocabulary of calculus to describe functions and sketch
        graphs of functions based on those descriptions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Key vocabulary phrases:
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> is <term>increasing on an interval</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> is <term>decreasing on an interval</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave up on an interval</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> is <term>concave down on an interval</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has a <term>local maximum at a point</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has a <term>local minimum at a point</term>.</p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)</m> has an <term>inflection point at a point</term>.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      What do I need to understand?
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following number lines indicate intervals on which <m>f(x)</m> has
          particular behaviors, with one number line shows where it is increasing or decreasing and the other number line shows where it is concave up or concave down.
        </p>
        <image label="F5-image-behavior-chart" width="90%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two number lines. The first has break points at -2 and 2: decreasing to the left of -2,
            increasing from -2 to 2, and decreasing to the right of 2.
            The second has break points at -4, 0, and 4: concave down to the left of -4,
            concave up from -4 to 0, concave down from 0 to 4, and concave up to the right of 4.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm, y=1cm, font=\small]
              % Increasing and decreasing
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,1.5) -- (8,1.5);
              \draw (-2,1.65) -- (-2,1.35) node[below] {$-2$};
              \draw (2,1.65) -- (2,1.35) node[below] {$2$};
              \node[above] at (-5,1.6) {decr};
              \node[above] at (0,1.6) {incr};
              \node[above] at (5,1.6) {decr};
              % Concavity
              \draw[thick, <->] (-8,0) -- (8,0);
              \draw (-4,0.15) -- (-4,-0.15) node[below] {$-4$};
              \draw (0,0.15) -- (0,-0.15) node[below] {$0$};
              \draw (4,0.15) -- (4,-0.15) node[below] {$4$};
              \node[above] at (-6,0.1) {c. down};
              \node[above] at (-2,0.1) {c. up};
              \node[above] at (2,0.1) {c. down};
              \node[above] at (6,0.1) {c. up};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph that accurately and clearly illustrates the behavior.
          Indicate on the graph points (including <m>x</m> values) where <m>f(x)</m>
          has local extreme values and inflection points.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We work from left to right to identify every interval shape.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The first change on either number line occurs at <m>x=-4</m>, so our first interval is <m>(-\infty, -4)</m>. The function is decreasing and concave down on <m>(-\infty,-4)</m>, which matches the shape on the top right quadrant of a circle. Just to the right of <m>x=-4</m>, the graph changes to concave up so <m>f(x)</m> has an inflection point at <m>x=-4</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The second change on either number line occurs at <m>x=-2</m>, so the next interval is <m>(-4, -2)</m>. The function is decreasing and concave up on <m>(-4,-2)</m>, which matches the shape on the bottom left quadrant of a circle. Just to the right of <m>x=-2</m>, the graph changes to increasing. Because <m>f(x)</m> changes from decreasing to increasing, <m>f(x)</m> has a local minimum at <m>x=-2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The next change occurs at <m>x=0</m>. <m>f(x)</m> is increasing and concave up on <m>(-2,0)</m>, which matches the shape in the bottom right quadrant of a circle. To the right of <m>x=0</m> the function changes being to concave down. This means <m>f(x)</m> has another inflection point at <m>x=0</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The next change is at <m>x=2</m>. <m>f(x)</m> is increasing and concave down on <m>(0,2)</m>, which matches the shape in the top left quadrant of a circle. To the right of <m>x=2</m> the function changes to decreasing. This means <m>f(x)</m> has a local maximum at <m>x=2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The last change is at <m>x=4</m>. <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing and concave down on <m>(2,4)</m>, and to the right of <m>x=4</m> the function changes to being concave up. This means <m>f(x)</m> has a third inflection point at <m>x=4</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          We can draw a graph by joining the different shapes at the points where shape was identified as changing.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <image label="F5-image-behavior-graph" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
              A smooth graph over a labeled x-axis with no y-axis and no y-values. For x less than -4 the curve
              decreases and is concave down. At x = -4 the concavity changes to concave up, and the curve keeps decreasing
              to a local minimum at x = -2. It then increases, with an inflection point at x = 0 where the concavity
              changes to concave down, up to a local maximum at x = 2. After that it decreases, with an inflection point
              at x = 4 where the concavity changes to concave up, and it continues decreasing. The three inflection
              points are at different heights. Points are marked at x = -4, -2, 0, 2, and 4.
            </shortdescription>
            <!-- Local min at x = -2, local max at x = 2; inflection points at x = -4, 0, 4, all at different heights.
                 The x-axis is drawn below the curve on purpose: no y-axis, no y-values, and no x-intercepts are shown.
                 Generated from f' built from smoothstep pieces with zeros at -2 and 2 and extrema of f' at -4, 0, 4. -->
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[
              \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm, y=1.4cm, >=stealth]
                % x-axis only, drawn below the curve (no y-axis, no y-values)
                \draw[->] (-6.6,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \foreach \x in {-4,-2,0,2,4} {
                  \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05);
                  \node[below] at (\x,-0.05) {\small $\x$};
                }
                % vertical guides from each marked point to its x-value
                \draw[dashed] (-4,0) -- (-4,1.382);
                \draw[dashed] (-2,0) -- (-2,0.300);
                \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (0,1.748);
                \draw[dashed] (2,0) -- (2,2.925);
                \draw[dashed] (4,0) -- (4,2.263);
                % curve
                \draw[thick] plot coordinates {
                  (-6.20,2.716) (-6.00,2.662) (-5.80,2.589) (-5.60,2.498) (-5.40,2.392) (-5.20,2.273)
                  (-5.00,2.142) (-4.80,2.001) (-4.60,1.853) (-4.40,1.699) (-4.20,1.542) (-4.00,1.382)
                  (-3.80,1.222) (-3.60,1.065) (-3.40,0.912) (-3.20,0.769) (-3.00,0.638) (-2.80,0.524)
                  (-2.60,0.430) (-2.40,0.359) (-2.20,0.315) (-2.00,0.300) (-1.80,0.316) (-1.60,0.363)
                  (-1.40,0.443) (-1.20,0.555) (-1.00,0.697) (-0.80,0.867) (-0.60,1.063) (-0.40,1.279)
                  (-0.20,1.509) (0.00,1.748) (0.20,1.979) (0.40,2.187) (0.60,2.368) (0.80,2.523)
                  (1.00,2.651) (1.20,2.754) (1.40,2.831) (1.60,2.884) (1.80,2.915) (2.00,2.925)
                  (2.20,2.915) (2.40,2.888) (2.60,2.846) (2.80,2.789) (3.00,2.720) (3.20,2.641)
                  (3.40,2.554) (3.60,2.460) (3.80,2.363) (4.00,2.263) (4.20,2.163) (4.40,2.064)
                  (4.60,1.968) (4.80,1.875) (5.00,1.786) (5.20,1.703) (5.40,1.627) (5.60,1.559)
                  (5.80,1.499) (6.00,1.450) (6.20,1.411)
                };
                % local extrema and inflection points
                \filldraw (-4,1.382) circle (2.2pt);
                \filldraw (-2,0.300) circle (2.2pt);
                \filldraw (0,1.748) circle (2.2pt);
                \filldraw (2,2.925) circle (2.2pt);
                \filldraw (4,2.263) circle (2.2pt);
              \end{tikzpicture}
              ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
